timestamp=2026-11-04T16:36:04.413Z level=INFO run=cbaab588 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\n\ncompanies = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/companies.csv')\ninvoices = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/invoices.csv')\n\n# Also 6453 we saw had very anomalous Benford (leading digits odd pattern)? 6453 had high Benford dev but different - not all 9s. \n# But 6453 is selling almost entirely to external recipients, large amounts relative to declared rev (303115 vs 109006 rev) - that’s (c).\n\n# Combine all signals.\n\n# Candidates from (a): Benford anomalies - especially 4612 and 8382 (all amounts start with 9, round numbers ~999, weird pattern, 25 identical-structure invoices)\ncand_a = [4612, 8382]\n# Others with high dev but less extreme - maybe include? Or focus on strongest signals.\n\n# Candidates from (b): companies that appear only as buyers despite having weak operating fundamentals\n# The 7 we identified earlier (bank_age small, new registration, small emp, low rev) who are only buyers\ncand_b = [1060, 3495, 4521, 7021, 8285, 8770, 8810]\n\n# Candidates from (c): sellers whose payments to external recipients look outsized relative to their declared business footprint\n# 2029 and 6453: selling to external buyers (not in companies register), amounts > declared annual revenue\ncand_c = [2029, 6453]\n\n# Also 3554, 2950, 1967: tiny companies with massive purchases from just 2 sellers - looks like conduit/round-tripping? \n# They are buyers only in effect? 3554 sells 1 invoice for 115, buys 10 invoices for 274K - mostly a buyer. And very weak fundamentals (emp=1, rev ~6K). \n# These fit (b) in spirit - appear mostly as buyers with weak fundamentals. Also possible (c) in terms of scale mismatch.\ncand_b2 = [3554, 2950, 1967]\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
